  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 126 AM MDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nHigh pressure will get squashed southward a bit into Friday with the \ndrier airmass of Thursday translating farther east across remaining \nNM zones. This will continue to suppress convection, but deeper \nsubtropical moisture in AZ would start to re-enter far western NM by \nlate in the day. Low chances for storms will be confined along the \nNM-AZ border while much lower dewpoints and hotter(and potentially \nrecord breaking) temperatures are observed farther east. \n\nFriday's moisture in AZ would start to seep farther east \nnortheastward into NM on Saturday as the upper high nudges that way \nin response to another deep low entering the Pacific Northwest \nstates. This will reacquaint the western and central mountains of NM \nwith isolated to scattered thunderstorms Saturday. \n\nThe Pacific Northwest low will lift into the northern Great Plains \nSunday into Monday of next week, only to be replaced by another \noffshore one in its wake Tuesday. Through this time the upper high \nwill slowly strengthen over the southeastern US, keeping a \nsouthwesterly wind component aloft over NM which will feed faint and \nheavily modified subtropical moisture into our state. PWATs from the \nLREF ensemble blend are generally advertised to range from 0.75 to \n1.0 inch in the northwestern half of the state rising to 1.1 to 1.2 \ninches in the southeastern half. POPs will increase through this \nSunday to Tuesday time frame, but will still generally be limited to \nscattered (30-50%) coverage. Temperatures will start to reduce \ncloser to climatology through this time as well.",
